title: Polymarket / DebugBear RUM Drainer
page: breaches/polymarket-debugbear-rum-drainer.html
as-of: 2026-11-21
promise: On October 29, 2026, a vendor's monitoring script on polymarket.com was swapped for a drainer that, per Polymarket, used traders' active sessions to sell positions and move funds.

== At a glance
layout: stats
stat: $3.9M | being returned to traders, per Polymarket | $2.7M taken in the attack plus $1.2M in trading-pause losses
stat: 726 | wallets affected, Polymarket's count | as reported by Bankless
stat: ~$2.6M | received by the attacker's wallet, in POL | 28,636,284 POL, traced on-chain by Ohey and Report URI
The positions taken from 33 deposit wallets are not in the POL figure. No source has named who did it.
notes:
Start with the three figures the page leads with, and with who stands behind each one.

Polymarket says it is returning $3.9M to traders: $2.7M taken in the attack plus $1.2M in trading-pause losses.

726 is the number of wallets affected. It is Polymarket's count, as reported by Bankless.

On-chain, Ohey and Report URI trace 28,636,284 POL, about $2.6M, to the attacker's wallet. POL is the native token of the Polygon network, and the drainer swapped the cash it took into POL. The positions taken from 33 deposit wallets are not in that figure.

The threat actor is unattributed. No source has named who did it.

Keep the attribution with each figure when you say it aloud. A later slide sets the published counts side by side, because they measure different things.

== What happened on October 29, 2026
layout: timeline
event: 16:54:57 UTC | Script host failing: Cloudflare 521, from Ohey's timeline
event: 17:10:23 UTC | The first POL reaches the recipient wallet
event: 17:59:20 UTC | A Polymarket build removes the DebugBear RUM script
event: 18:05 UTC | Cancel-only mode; relayer actions paused
event: 18:28 to 18:38 UTC | The recipient sends all its POL on to three wallets
event: October 30 | Polymarket's status-page postmortem
17:59:20 is a build time, not a verified deployment time, per Hudson Rock. Spacing is not to scale.
notes:
Every time on this slide is one the page gives, in UTC on October 29, 2026. The markers are evenly spaced, and the gaps between them are not to scale.

At 16:54:57, an archived response in Ohey's timeline shows Cloudflare returning error 521, origin not responding, for cdn.debugbear.com, the host Polymarket loaded the script from. The first POL reached the recipient wallet at 17:10:23.

A Polymarket build carrying the commit "remove debugbear rum script" is timestamped 17:59:20. Hudson Rock notes that is a build time, not a verified deployment time. At 18:05, Polymarket put the exchange in cancel-only mode and paused relayer actions, per Ohey citing Unchained.

Between 18:28 and 18:38, the recipient sent all its POL on to three wallets, per Ohey.

Polymarket's status-page postmortem came on October 30. The stage 07 slides explain why the page relies on build, on-chain and press times rather than the status page's own.

== Polymarket's postmortem names no vendor
layout: table
row: Record | What it shows
row: Polymarket's postmortem, October 30 | The attack, without naming the vendor
row: Hudson Rock | The drainer, retrieved from cdn.debugbear.com
row: A Polymarket build that afternoon | A commit removing the "debugbear rum script"
row: DebugBear's first notice | RUM customers asked to remove the embed code
row: DebugBear's follow-up | Says its DNS records were compromised through its domain registrar
DebugBear has not named the registrar or said how the records were changed. RUM stands for real user monitoring.
notes:
Polymarket's own postmortem, published on its status page on October 30, describes the attack and does not name the vendor. The page draws on the other records shown here.

Hudson Rock retrieved the drainer from cdn.debugbear.com. A Polymarket build that afternoon carried a commit removing the "debugbear rum script". DebugBear's first notice on October 29 said it was impacted by an ongoing security incident and asked its RUM customers to remove the embed code.

RUM stands for real user monitoring. The page describes the script polymarket.com loaded from the vendor as a performance-monitoring script.

DebugBear later gave the cause: "Our DNS server records were compromised through our domain registrar." That explanation is DebugBear's own. It has not named the registrar or said how the records were changed, and the page keeps that caveat next to its headline.

== Two caveats the page keeps next to its headline
layout: compare
col: The cause is DebugBear's account | DNS records compromised through its domain registrar, DebugBear says | It has not named the registrar | Or said how the records were changed
col: What the $3.9M includes | $1.2M of compensation for losses during the trading pause | On-chain analysts trace 28,636,284 POL, about $2.6M, to the attacker's wallet | That excludes the positions the attacker also took
The timing evidence fits DebugBear's account without proving it.
notes:
The page puts two caveats next to its headline, and this slide keeps them together.

The first is the cause. DebugBear says its DNS records were compromised through its domain registrar. That explanation is DebugBear's own: it has not named the registrar or said how the records were changed. The page says the timing evidence fits that account without proving it, and the stage 01 slide shows that evidence.

The second is the money. Polymarket says it is returning $3.9M to traders, and that figure includes $1.2M of compensation for losses during the trading pause. On-chain analysts trace 28,636,284 POL, about $2.6M, to the attacker's wallet, not counting the positions the attacker also took.

When you quote the $3.9M, say what it includes.

== The published counts measure different things
layout: table
row: Who counted | What they report
row: Polymarket, via Bankless and CircleID | $2.7M taken from 726 wallets
row: TheFatMbappe, cited by Ohey | 2.724M pUSD from 721 wallets
row: Bitquery, Polygon data to 19:49 UTC | $2.73M from 737 accounts, pUSD and USDC at $1
row: Ohey by 18:15; Report URI at 18:19:58 UTC | 28,636,284 POL at the recipient; about $2.6M, per Report URI
row: PolygonScan screenshot supplied to Hudson Rock | About $2.19M; not a verified total, per Hudson Rock
Times are UTC on October 29. The POL figure leaves out the positions taken; Bitquery says the attacker held a little less in POL after swap costs.
notes:
The page sets these counts side by side and says they measure different things.

Polymarket reports $2.7M taken from 726 wallets, as relayed by Bankless and CircleID. TheFatMbappe, cited by Ohey, traced 2.724M pUSD from 721 wallets. Bitquery read Polygon data up to 19:49 UTC, counted pUSD and USDC at $1 each, and found $2.73M taken from 737 accounts. It says the attacker held a little less in POL after swap costs. USDC is a stablecoin pegged to the US dollar.

Ohey has the recipient holding 28,636,284 POL by 18:15 UTC. Report URI gives the same figure at 18:19:58 UTC and values it at about $2.6M. That counts POL only, not the positions taken.

A PolygonScan screenshot supplied to Hudson Rock showed about $2.19M at the recipient, which Hudson Rock says is not a verified total of stolen funds. PolygonScan is a block explorer for the Polygon network.

When someone asks how much was stolen, name the source and what it counted.

== The kill chain on one slide
layout: flow
step: 01 | Resource Development | Vendor's domain | T1584.001
step: 02 | Initial Access | Same URL, new bytes | T1195.002
step: 03 | Execution | Waits for a login | T1059.007
step: 04 | Collection | Live session borrowed | T1185
step: 05 | Exfiltration | Vendor's data host | T1041
step: 06 | Impact | Sold and swept | T1657
step: 07 | Response | Pulled and paused | T1657
Arrows show the page's stage order. Stage 01's cause is DebugBear's own account.
notes:
Here is the whole chain on one slide: the page's seven stages, each with its phase label and ATT&CK technique.

Stage 01, Resource Development, is DebugBear's domain, where DebugBear says its DNS records were compromised through its registrar. Stage 02, Initial Access, is the script URL Polymarket trusted serving a drainer. Stage 03, Execution, is the drainer checking for a logged-in trader. Stage 04, Collection, is the drainer finding Polymarket's order client and acting through the user's live session. Stage 05, Exfiltration, is the reporting to the vendor's data host. Stage 06, Impact, is positions sold or transferred and cash swept through Polymarket's relayer. Stage 07, Response, is the script pulled, trading paused and sessions reset. The page maps stages 06 and 07 to the same technique, T1657, Financial Theft.

Read the arrows as the page's stage order. The step from 01 to 02 rests on DebugBear's account, which the page says the timing evidence fits without proving.

== DebugBear says its DNS records were compromised through its registrar
layout: stage
stage: 01
phase: Resource Development
technique: T1584.001 | Compromise Infrastructure: Domains
fact: Registrar | not named; DebugBear has not said how the records changed
fact: Cloudflare 521 | 16:54:57 UTC on October 29, per Ohey's archived response
fact: Registry change | nine minutes before the host failed, per RuntimeWire citing Kirkland; not in Ohey's write-up
The timing evidence fits DebugBear's account without proving it.
notes:
DebugBear's first notice on October 29 said it was impacted by an ongoing security incident. A follow-up post gave the cause: "Our DNS server records were compromised through our domain registrar." The page maps this stage to T1584.001, Compromise Infrastructure: Domains. DNS, the Domain Name System, maps names like cdn.debugbear.com to servers.

In the same post DebugBear said it could not receive email sent to its domain, that its initial investigation showed the attack targeted the website of one specific customer, and that it had seen no indication of a compromise of its internal systems while it continued to investigate.

An archived response in Ohey's timeline shows Cloudflare returning error 521, origin not responding, for cdn.debugbear.com at 16:54:57 UTC. RuntimeWire reports, citing Ohey's founder Matt Kirkland, that the registry record for debugbear.com changed nine minutes before the script host began failing. The only source RuntimeWire lists is Ohey's write-up, which does not mention a registry change, and RuntimeWire says the change's details and cause remain unknown.

CircleID, covering a separate registrar-level hijacking campaign against 12 cryptocurrency sites, says no common attacker, registrar or technical cause has been established between that campaign and this incident.

== What ATT&CK calls compromising domains
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK T1584.001 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1584/001/
Domain registration hijacking changes a domain's registration without the original registrant's permission. Routes include social engineering a registration help desk, renewal process gaps or a compromised cloud service that manages domains. The page does not say how DebugBear's records changed.
notes:
Background, from MITRE ATT&CK, in general terms.

ATT&CK files this under Compromise Infrastructure: Domains, in the Resource Development tactic. Adversaries may hijack domains and subdomains to use during targeting. Domain registration hijacking is changing the registration of a domain name without the permission of the original registrant.

ATT&CK's examples of how: gaining access to the email account of the person listed as the domain's owner and then claiming a forgotten password; social engineering a domain registration help desk; taking advantage of renewal process gaps; or compromising a cloud service that manages domains, such as AWS Route53.

It also describes subdomain hijacking, where DNS entries point to resources that no longer exist, and domain shadowing, where an adversary who controls a domain adds malicious subdomains while keeping the existing DNS records.

These are general examples. The page does not say how DebugBear's records were changed, and DebugBear has not said.

== The script URL Polymarket trusted began serving a drainer
layout: stage
stage: 02
phase: Initial Access
technique: T1195.002 | Supply Chain Compromise: Compromise Software Supply Chain
fact: URL | cdn.debugbear[.]com/C7xcKKrjUnXK.js, which never changed
fact: Genuine file | 60,493 bytes, served from Google Cloud Storage
fact: Drainer | 15,187 bytes, served by nginx
fact: Cloudflare cache | Chicago edge, 17:27:45 UTC on October 29, per Ohey
Scott Helme concludes a different server from the usual bucket answered.
notes:
Polymarket loaded cdn.debugbear.com/C7xcKKrjUnXK.js, and the URL never changed. On the slide it is written defanged, with [.], so it cannot be clicked. The page maps this stage to T1195.002, Compromise Software Supply Chain.

Report URI compared the responses. Before the attack, the file came from Google Cloud Storage, and every response carried Google's markers, such as x-goog-hash. During it, those markers were gone and the response carried an nginx-format ETag. From that, Scott Helme concludes that whatever answered for cdn.debugbear.com was a different server from the bucket that normally serves the file. nginx is a widely used web server.

Ohey records Cloudflare's Chicago edge caching the drainer at 17:27:45 UTC. An edge is one of a content delivery network's servers close to users.

The page also gives the drainer's SHA-256 hash. The next slide sets the two responses side by side.

== One URL, two responses
layout: compare
col: Before the attack | The genuine RUM file, 60,493 bytes | Served from Google Cloud Storage | Google's markers, such as x-goog-hash, on every response, per Report URI
col: During the attack | The drainer, 15,187 bytes | Google's markers gone, per Report URI | An nginx-format ETag, which Helme decodes to October 27, 12:50:43 UTC
That date is Helme's reading of a header from the drainer's server. Hudson Rock does not treat that server-supplied value as the start of the attack.
notes:
This slide sets out the evidence the page draws from captured responses.

Before the attack, the genuine RUM file was 60,493 bytes and came from Google Cloud Storage. Report URI found Google's markers, such as x-goog-hash, on every response. x-goog-hash is a header Google Cloud Storage sends carrying an object's checksums.

During the attack, the URL served a 15,187-byte drainer. Google's markers were gone, and the response carried an nginx-format ETag. An ETag is a header a server sends to label a version of a file. Scott Helme decodes that ETag to a modification time of October 27, 2026 at 12:50:43 UTC.

Keep the caveats with that date. It is Helme's reading of a header sent by the server that served the drainer, and Ohey lists the same date from the same server. Hudson Rock saw the same date in the response's Last-Modified header and does not treat that server-supplied value as the start of the attack. So do not count back from October 27 to say when the attack began.

== What Subresource Integrity checks
layout: background
source: OWASP Third Party JavaScript Management Cheat Sheet | https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Third_Party_Javascript_Management_Cheat_Sheet.html
OWASP warns there is usually no guarantee that code a third party hosts stays the same as what your developers tested. With Subresource Integrity, the script element carries integrity metadata for the vendor's file, so only reviewed code runs.
notes:
Background, from OWASP's Third Party JavaScript Management Cheat Sheet, in general terms.

OWASP notes there is usually no guarantee that the code a third party hosts will remain the same as what your developers and testers saw. It also notes that third-party code runs with the exact same privileges granted to the user, similar to cross-site scripting.

With Subresource Integrity, the developer generates integrity metadata for the vendor's JavaScript and adds it to the script element as an integrity attribute, a value such as sha384 followed by the hash. OWASP says this ensures that only the code that has been reviewed is executed.

Two practical points from OWASP: SRI needs CORS enabled on the vendor's host, and a vendor update can leave you with secure but non-working code. CORS, cross-origin resource sharing, is how a server tells browsers that other sites may read its responses.

== The drainer exited unless three Polymarket login keys were in localStorage
layout: stage
stage: 03
phase: Execution
technique: T1059.007 | Command and Scripting Interpreter: JavaScript
fact: Keys read | polymarket.auth.params, polymarket.auth.proxyWallet, poly_clob_api_key_map
fact: Fallback config | recipient 0x3F99...fc18, $10 minimums, paid in POL, per Ohey
fact: Secrets sent out | none identified by Hudson Rock
The drainer acted only for visitors with a logged-in session.
notes:
The script fetched its configuration from data.debugbear.com, checked the visitor's location against Polymarket's geoblock endpoint and skipped blocked regions. The page maps this stage to T1059.007, JavaScript.

It then read three keys that Polymarket's app writes to localStorage, the browser's storage for each site: polymarket.auth.params, the login type and wallet address; polymarket.auth.proxyWallet, the deposit wallet; and poly_clob_api_key_map, the trading API credentials. Without them it stopped. With them it sorted the account as a browser wallet, labelled eoa, or an email login, labelled magic. EOA stands for externally owned account. Ohey, Hudson Rock and Report URI describe the same gate.

If the configuration request failed, the script fell back to hard-coded values, per Ohey: the recipient address beginning 0x3F99, a $10 minimum per account and per position, and POL as the payout coin.

Hudson Rock found that the script reads the trading API credential map, but it did not identify a routine that sends that map, a password, a seed phrase or a private key to the reporting endpoint.

The next slide explains localStorage in general.

== What localStorage exposes to page scripts
layout: background
source: OWASP HTML5 Security Cheat Sheet | https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/HTML5_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html
OWASP says data in localStorage is always accessible by JavaScript, and every object is shared within an origin, protected by the Same Origin Policy. A single cross-site scripting flaw can steal all the data in it.
notes:
Background, from OWASP's HTML5 Security Cheat Sheet, in general terms.

Local storage, also called web storage, lets a site keep data in the browser. OWASP notes that every object is shared within an origin and protected with the Same Origin Policy, and that there is no way to limit an object to a path, as a cookie's path attribute can. An origin is the combination of scheme, host and port a page comes from.

OWASP says the data is always accessible by JavaScript. It warns that a single cross-site scripting flaw can steal all the data in these objects, or load malicious data into them, so their contents should not be considered trusted.

== The drainer found Polymarket's order client and acted through the live session
layout: stage
stage: 04
phase: Collection
technique: T1185 | Browser Session Hijacking
fact: React fiber search | up to 400 elements, 5 seconds or 60,000 nodes, per Ohey
fact: Match | postOrder plus createOrder or createMarketOrder, with a signer
fact: If not mounted | 15 retries at 250 ms, then a page reload
fact: Keys of its own | none
Hudson Rock: a request in the code does not by itself show a silent signature.
notes:
The drainer held no keys of its own. Ohey describes it scanning up to 400 buttons, links and containers for React fiber objects, walking the tree for up to five seconds or 60,000 nodes, and keeping any object with a postOrder method and a createOrder or createMarketOrder method. That object is Polymarket's order client, with its signer attached. If the client was not yet mounted, the script retried 15 times at 250 ms intervals and then reloaded the page at the user's wallet path. The page maps this stage to T1185, Browser Session Hijacking.

React is a JavaScript library for building user interfaces, and a fiber is React's internal record of a component on the page. A signer is what produces signatures on the user's behalf.

Polymarket's postmortem says the script "used their active session to sell positions and move funds out of their wallet." Hudson Rock adds a caution about reading the code: the script requested signatures from the existing signer, and a wallet provider may prompt or reject, so a request in the code does not by itself show a silent signature. The page says the on-chain transfers in stage 06 show that many of the requests went through.

== Victim profiles went to data.debugbear.com, a host the CSP already allowed
layout: stage
stage: 05
phase: Exfiltration
technique: T1041 | Exfiltration Over C2 Channel
fact: Endpoints | init, submit, update and handle under /metrics/, per Ohey
fact: Sent | victim profile, portfolio and transaction hashes
fact: connect-src | data.debugbear.com, *.polymarket.com, *.alchemy.com, *.fun.xyz
fact: CSP reporting | none visible in the archived copy
As Helme puts it, "Only the bytes of the script changed."
notes:
Ohey lists four endpoints on the vendor's data host: /metrics/init for configuration, /metrics/submit for the portfolio and each position before any transfer, /metrics/update for each cash sweep and /metrics/handle for each position transfer. Every report carried the deposit and login wallets, login type, operating system, a tracking ID, and the visitor's IP address and country taken from Polymarket's geoblock response. The page maps this stage to T1041, Exfiltration Over C2 Channel; C2 means command and control.

Report URI reconstructed Polymarket's Content Security Policy, its CSP, from an archived copy of the homepage taken on the morning of the attack. script-src-elem allowed https://cdn.debugbear.com, and connect-src allowed https://data.debugbear.com along with *.polymarket.com, *.alchemy.com and *.fun.xyz. Report URI says that covers every request the drainer made, and Ohey says every host the drainer called other than DebugBear's was on the policy. No report-uri or report-to directive was visible, so a violation would not have been reported.

As Scott Helme puts it, "Only the bytes of the script changed."

The next slide explains what a CSP controls, in general.

== What a Content Security Policy controls
layout: background
source: OWASP Content Security Policy Cheat Sheet | https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Content_Security_Policy_Cheat_Sheet.html
A CSP header tells the browser where a page may load scripts from (script-src, script-src-elem) and where its code may connect (connect-src). Reporting directives, report-to and the deprecated report-uri, send violations to an endpoint.
notes:
Background, from OWASP's Content Security Policy Cheat Sheet, in general terms.

A Content Security Policy is a response header that tells the browser what a page may load and run. OWASP calls it defense in depth: a second layer, not the only defense against cross-site scripting.

Fetch directives name trusted locations. script-src sets where scripts can execute from, and script-src-elem controls script requests and blocks. connect-src covers fetch requests, XHR, eventsource, beacon and websocket connections.

Reporting directives deliver violations to a chosen location and do nothing on their own. report-to is the current directive; report-uri is deprecated, and OWASP suggests declaring both for older browsers. A Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only header sends reports without blocking, often as a step before enforcing.

OWASP adds that a CSP can enforce Subresource Integrity, which can help prevent malicious code from loading if a third-party host of JavaScript files, such as analytics scripts, is compromised.

== Positions were sold or transferred and cash swept through Polymarket's relayer
layout: stage
stage: 06
phase: Impact
technique: T1657 | Financial Theft
fact: Browser wallets | positions worth over $10, in one safeBatchTransferFrom call
fact: Email logins | sold at 97% of price, then 50%, then the minimum tick
fact: Cash | swapped to POL, posted to Polymarket's relayer, per Ohey
fact: October 29, UTC | first POL 17:10:23; position transfers 17:26:29 to 18:02:17
All of it was submitted through Polymarket's own relayer.
notes:
For browser-wallet accounts, every position worth more than $10 went to the recipient in one safeBatchTransferFrom call on the conditional-token contract. For email-login accounts, positions were sold with fill-and-kill orders at 97% of the current price, then 50%, then the market's minimum tick, and the proceeds joined the cash. A fill-and-kill order fills what it can at once and cancels the rest. The page maps this stage to T1657, Financial Theft.

Ohey describes the cash being swapped to POL using quotes from api.fun.xyz, wrapped in EIP-712 DepositWallet batches with a one-hour deadline and posted to Polymarket's relayer. EIP-712 is an Ethereum standard for signing structured data. Report URI also names Relay and Polymarket's Collateral Offramp in the conversion path.

The first POL reached the recipient at 17:10:23 UTC. Position tokens followed from 17:26:29 to 18:02:17 UTC in 36 transfers from 33 deposit wallets, which Report URI says were sent by 31 relayer addresses. By 18:15 UTC the recipient held 28,636,284 POL, per Ohey; Report URI gives the same figure at 18:19:58 UTC and values it at about $2.6M.

== October 29: script pulled and trading paused within minutes of 18:00 UTC
layout: stage
stage: 07
phase: Response
technique: T1657 | Financial Theft
fact: Removal build time | 17:59:20 UTC; not a verified deployment time, per Hudson Rock
fact: Cancel-only | 18:05 UTC, relayer paused too, per Ohey citing Unchained
fact: POL moved on | to three wallets, 18:28 to 18:38 UTC, per Ohey
fact: Postmortem | caches cleared, sessions reset, law enforcement referral
One more account was drained at 19:11 UTC, after the relayer resumed, per Bitquery.
notes:
A Polymarket build carrying the commit "remove debugbear rum script" is timestamped 17:59:20 UTC, which Hudson Rock notes is a build time and not a verified deployment time. At 18:05 UTC Polymarket put the exchange in cancel-only mode and paused relayer actions, per Ohey citing Unchained; BleepingComputer gives 2:05 p.m. ET. The page maps this stage to T1657, Financial Theft, as it does stage 06.

Polymarket's postmortem lists what it did: it removed the script and cleared every cache, paused trading and the relayer until the site was clean, reset all active login sessions and referred the case to law enforcement. It is redeploying third-party scripts with additional integrity controls and continuous monitoring and verification, and gives stronger CSP, explicit version pinning and sandboxing as examples.

It is returning $3.9M to traders, and said that by the afternoon of October 30, 95% of affected wallets had been refunded in full. Bankless reports that the $1.19M goes to roughly 5,500 wallets that lost out during the pause or had positions taken, and that larger victims reportedly need to sign a release to be repaid. Terms users shared, including ID checks and a broad non-disparagement clause, drew pushback, and Polymarket had not publicly addressed them.

== The response on October 29, by build, on-chain and press times
layout: timeline
event: 17:59:20 UTC | Build removing the script; a build time, per Hudson Rock
event: 18:00:58 UTC | DebugBear posts its notice, per Ohey
event: 18:05 UTC | Cancel-only mode; relayer actions paused
event: About 18:10 UTC | Last drain of the hour, per Bitquery
event: 18:52 UTC | Relayer back; trading fully back at about 19:00
event: 19:11 UTC | One more account drained through the relayer, per Bitquery
Polymarket's status entry, created October 30 and backdated, says Identified 17:38 and Resolved 18:06 UTC. BleepingComputer gave 19:05 UTC for the resolution.
notes:
The page builds this stage's timeline from build, on-chain and press times, and this slide follows it. The markers are evenly spaced, and the gaps are not to scale.

The removal build is timestamped 17:59:20, a build time rather than a verified deployment time, per Hudson Rock. DebugBear posted its notice at 18:00:58, per Ohey. At 18:05 the exchange went to cancel-only mode and relayer actions paused. Bitquery found the last drain of the hour at about 18:10, with Polymarket's relayer sending its last transaction 30 seconds later, and Hudson Rock's homepage capture at 18:12:06 no longer contained the DebugBear filename. The relayer came back at 18:52, trading was fully back at about 19:00, and one more account was drained through the relayer at 19:11, per Bitquery.

The page explains why it does not use the status page's times. The status page now carries an entry created on October 30 and backdated to October 29, with Identified at 17:38 UTC and Resolved at 18:06 UTC. The status incident BleepingComputer linked on October 29 no longer resolves, and BleepingComputer gave 3:05 p.m. ET, 19:05 UTC, for the resolution.

Earlier, between 17:45 and 17:56, the position tokens had gone to a fourth wallet in 25 transfers, per Ohey. BleepingComputer valued the POL sent on to three wallets at about $2.57M.

== A matching shape and two separate incidents
layout: table
row: Incident | What happened | Its tie to this incident
row: Polyfill.io, 2024 | A widely embedded script's domain was sold; its new owner served malicious code | The shape matches, per the page; DebugBear has described no change of ownership
row: Polymarket, disclosed June 25, 2026 | A separate vendor-script injection, about $3M, as Decrypt reported | cside: the shared pattern does not establish the same attacker or method
row: 12 cryptocurrency sites | A separate registrar-level hijacking campaign, per CircleID | CircleID: no common attacker, registrar or technical cause established
Polyfill.io has its own csoh.org kill chain, linked at the end of this deck.
notes:
The page draws one comparison and keeps two other incidents apart from this one.

The shape matches Polyfill.io in 2024, where the domain behind a widely embedded script was sold and its new owner served malicious code to every site still loading it. Here, DebugBear says its DNS records were compromised through its domain registrar, and it has not described any change of ownership.

Polymarket had disclosed a separate vendor-script injection on June 25, 2026, which Decrypt reported at about $3M. cside notes that the shared pattern does not establish the same attacker or method.

CircleID, covering a separate registrar-level hijacking campaign against 12 cryptocurrency sites, says no common attacker, registrar or technical cause has been established between that campaign and this incident.

Neither source establishes a common attacker with this incident, and this deck does not suggest one.

== Seven defenses against seven stages
layout: matrix
columns: 01 | 02 | 03 | 04 | 05 | 06 | 07
row: Pin third-party scripts to a hash | 02 | 07
row: Keep scripts off session pages | 03 | 04
row: CSP as if any allowed host can turn hostile | 02 | 05
row: Fresh confirmation before code moves money | 04
row: Be able to stop the money path separately | 06 | 07
row: Reset sessions, revoke readable credentials | 03 | 07
row: Keep the bytes and headers served | 02
No defense on the page is tied to stage 01, DebugBear's domain.
notes:
This matrix sets the page's seven defenses against its seven stages. A cell is marked only where the defense's own words cite that stage's facts.

Pinning to a hash cites the genuine file and an address identical before, during and after the attack, stage 02, and the version pinning among Polymarket's fixes, which it says would not have stopped this swap, stage 07. Keeping scripts off session pages cites the login keys read from localStorage, stage 03, and the order client found in memory, stage 04. The CSP defense cites two files at the same URL, stage 02, and the missing reporting directive, stage 05. Fresh confirmation cites the drainer asking Polymarket's own signer: stage 04. Stopping the money path cites drains sent by the relayer, stage 06, and the pause and the 19:11 drain, stage 07. Resetting sessions cites the readable credential map, stage 03, and the session reset, stage 07. Keeping the bytes cites the two file sizes and the header switch: stage 02.

Column 01 is empty: no defense cites DebugBear's registrar, its DNS records or the 16:54 failure. That gap is a discussion question at the end.

== Pin third-party scripts on authenticated pages to a hash
layout: compare
col: Report URI's case for a hash | It computed the SHA-384 of the genuine file | Set as the integrity attribute, the browser would have refused the drainer | It notes that dynamic third-party scripts are hard to pin
col: Why a version is not enough | Polymarket now lists explicit version pinning among its fixes | The script's address was identical before, during and after the attack | Only a hash of the expected bytes tells the two files apart
Hudson Rock and cside recommend Subresource Integrity wherever an asset can be pinned, with a controlled process for approving updates.
notes:
The page's first defense is to pin third-party scripts on authenticated pages to a hash.

Report URI computed the SHA-384 of the genuine file and notes that with an integrity attribute set to it, the browser would have refused the drainer. It also notes that dynamic third-party scripts are hard to pin.

Polymarket now lists explicit version pinning among its fixes, but a version alone would not have stopped this swap. Report URI calls it the limit of any URL-based defense: the script's address was identical before, during and after the attack. A version in the URL does not help when the vendor's host is answering with different bytes; only a hash of the expected bytes tells the two files apart.

Hudson Rock and cside recommend Subresource Integrity wherever an asset can be pinned, with a controlled process for approving updates. SRI is the browser check the earlier background slide described.

== Write the CSP as if any allowed host can turn hostile
layout: compare
col: Polymarket's policy | Both DebugBear hostnames, so every request the drainer made was allowed | No reporting directive, so nothing would have been reported
col: What Report URI suggests | Add 'report-sha256' to script-src, so a changed file at a known URL can alert | Deploy Integrity-Policy-Report-Only to find scripts loaded without SRI, before enforcing it
An allowlist of hosts cannot tell two files at the same URL apart.
notes:
The page's next defense: write your CSP as if any allowed host can turn hostile, and switch on reporting.

Polymarket's policy listed both DebugBear hostnames, so every request the drainer made was allowed, and with no reporting directive nothing would have been reported. An allowlist of hosts cannot tell two files at the same URL apart.

Report URI suggests adding 'report-sha256' to script-src, so that a changed file at a known URL can raise an alert, and deploying Integrity-Policy-Report-Only to find scripts loaded without SRI before enforcing it. A report-only policy makes the browser report what it would have blocked, without blocking it.

== Limit what page code can reach, and what it can sign
layout: compare
col: Keep scripts off session pages | A monitoring script runs with the same reach as the page's own code | So this one read the login keys and found the order client | Hudson Rock and cside: remove unnecessary ones from those pages
col: Fresh confirmation to move money | The drainer needed no keys; it asked Polymarket's own signer | Hudson Rock: audit what page code can request from the signer | And which actions need fresh user confirmation; cside: the same review
cside adds that each remaining script should have a business reason, an owner and a removal procedure.
notes:
Two defenses limit what page code can do.

First, keep third-party scripts off pages that hold a trading or signing session. A monitoring script runs with the same reach as the page's own code, which is how this one read Polymarket's login keys from localStorage and found the order client in memory. Hudson Rock and cside both recommend removing unnecessary scripts from authenticated trading and signing pages, and cside adds that each remaining one should have a business reason, an owner and a removal procedure.

Second, require fresh, explicit confirmation before page code can move money. The drainer needed no keys because it asked Polymarket's own signer. Hudson Rock recommends auditing what page code can request from the signer and which actions need fresh user confirmation, and cside recommends the same review of signing authority.

== Stop the money path, then reset and revoke
layout: compare
col: Stop the path that moves money | Polymarket's postmortem: trading and relayer paused to stop further drains | Per Bitquery, every drain went through the relayer and stopped when it did | One more account was drained at 19:11 UTC, October 29, after the relayer resumed
col: Reset and revoke after removal | Polymarket's postmortem: every cache cleared, all active login sessions reset | The drainer could read the credential map; Hudson Rock found no code sending it | Search Engine Journal: revoke exposed API credentials; confirm old sessions are rejected
DeFi Prime: the removal could not reach browsers that already had the page loaded.
notes:
Two defenses are about the response.

First, be able to stop the path that moves money, separately from removing the script. Polymarket's postmortem says it paused trading and the relayer to stop further drains. Bitquery found that every drain was sent by Polymarket's relayer and that the drains stopped when the relayer did, about ten minutes after the removal build's timestamp; one more account was drained at 19:11 UTC after the relayer resumed. DeFi Prime notes that the removal could not reach browsers that already had the page loaded, so the cancel-only mode and relayer pause mattered as much as the code change.

Second, after removing a compromised script, reset sessions and revoke the credentials it could read. Polymarket's removal build is timestamped 17:59 UTC and its trading pause began at 18:05; its postmortem also lists clearing every cache and resetting all active login sessions, without times. The drainer could read the trading API credential map. Hudson Rock found no code that sent it out, but Search Engine Journal's checklist for affected site owners says to revoke exposed API credentials and confirm that old sessions are rejected, since a password change alone may not end them.

== Keep the bytes and headers your users were served
layout: compare
col: Where the evidence came from | Captured responses: two file sizes and two hashes | Headers that switched from Google Cloud Storage markers to nginx | DeFi Prime's fetch of the drainer from Cloudflare's cache at 18:13 UTC
col: What to keep | The exact response bytes, hashes and HTTP metadata, per Hudson Rock | The script contents delivered to users, per cside | So you can establish what ran and when it changed
On October 29, a cache-bypassing request shortly after 18:13 returned 404; by 20:37 UTC both DebugBear hostnames returned NXDOMAIN, per Ohey.
notes:
The last defense: keep the bytes and headers your users were served.

The evidence in this case came from captured responses: two file sizes, two hashes, and headers that switched from Google Cloud Storage markers to nginx. Hudson Rock recommends capturing the exact response bytes, hashes and HTTP metadata, and cside recommends preserving the script contents delivered to users, so you can establish what ran and when it changed.

On October 29, DeFi Prime fetched the drainer from Cloudflare's cache at 18:13 UTC, and a cache-bypassing request shortly afterwards returned 404. By 20:37 UTC both DebugBear hostnames returned NXDOMAIN, per Ohey. NXDOMAIN is the DNS answer meaning a name does not exist.

== Check your own environment
layout: checklist
check: Which third-party scripts run on pages that hold a trading or signing session?
check: Is each of those scripts pinned to a hash, with a process for updates?
check: Would your CSP report a changed file at an allowed URL?
check: Can page code ask your signer to move money without fresh confirmation?
check: Can you stop the path that moves money separately from a code change?
check: After removing a script, would you reset sessions and revoke what it could read?
check: Could you show the exact bytes and headers a script served your users?
notes:
Turn the page's defenses into questions about your own estate. Each question comes from one of them.

Ask which third-party scripts run on pages that hold a trading or signing session, and whether each has a business reason, an owner and a removal procedure. Ask whether those scripts are pinned to a hash with Subresource Integrity, and how updates get approved.

Ask whether your Content Security Policy would report anything if a file at an allowed URL changed. An allowlist of hosts cannot tell two files at the same URL apart.

Ask what page code can request from your signer, and which actions need fresh confirmation. Ask whether you can stop the path that moves money, the way Polymarket paused trading and its relayer, separately from a code change.

Ask whether your response plan resets sessions and revokes the credentials a removed script could read. Finally, ask whether you could show the exact bytes, hashes and headers a script served your users, and when they changed.

== Could anything you run tell two files at one URL apart?
layout: question
The script's address was identical before, during and after the attack, and Polymarket's CSP allowed both DebugBear hostnames.

Pick one third-party script on your site. What would notice if its bytes changed?
notes:
Give the room a minute with this one before moving on.

The page's facts: Polymarket loaded the same URL throughout, and the response switched from a 60,493-byte file served from Google Cloud Storage to a 15,187-byte drainer served by nginx. Polymarket's policy listed both DebugBear hostnames, so every request the drainer made was allowed, and no reporting directive was visible.

Ask each person to pick one third-party script in their own environment and say what, if anything, would notice a change in its bytes, before you show the answer slide.

== Only a hash of the expected bytes tells the files apart
layout: answer
A URL version does not help when the host serves different bytes. Report URI notes dynamic third-party scripts are hard to pin.
- Hudson Rock, cside: Subresource Integrity where assets can be pinned.
- Report URI: 'report-sha256' can flag a changed file.
notes:
The page's answer: only a hash of the expected bytes tells the two files apart.

The script's address was identical before, during and after the attack, so a version in the URL does not help when the vendor's host is answering with different bytes. Report URI computed the SHA-384 of the genuine file and notes that with an integrity attribute set to it, the browser would have refused the drainer. It also notes that dynamic third-party scripts are hard to pin. Hudson Rock and cside recommend Subresource Integrity wherever an asset can be pinned, with a controlled process for approving updates.

For detection, Report URI suggests adding 'report-sha256' to script-src so a changed file at a known URL can raise an alert.

Bring the group's examples back to this. The page's point is that the check has to look at the bytes, not the address.

== What would you add for stage 01?
layout: question
The page ties no defense to stage 01, where DebugBear says its DNS records were compromised through its domain registrar.

What would you add, and which reported detail would it catch?
notes:
This question comes from the gap in the defense matrix.

The page ties its seven defenses to stages 02 to 07. It ties none to stage 01, where DebugBear says its DNS records were compromised through its domain registrar. DebugBear has not named the registrar or said how the records were changed.

Let the group propose ideas before you show the answer slide. Ask each proposal to name the detail from the page that it would have caught.

== The page ties no defense to stage 01
layout: answer
Any control you propose for the vendor's domain is your own. Test it against what the page reports: an unnamed registrar, the 16:54:57 UTC failure on October 29, and the registry change RuntimeWire reports, citing Kirkland.
notes:
Be clear that the page stops here. It ties no defense to stage 01, and this deck adds none of its own.

The group may argue that pinning the script to a hash would have stopped the drainer whatever happened at the registrar. The page ties that defense to the swap at stage 02, not to the domain, so that argument is the group's own.

Test each proposal against the facts the page reports for stage 01: DebugBear's statement that its DNS records were compromised through its domain registrar, with the registrar unnamed and the method undisclosed; Cloudflare's error 521 for cdn.debugbear.com at 16:54:57 UTC on October 29, from an archived response in Ohey's timeline; and the registry record change nine minutes before the host failed, which RuntimeWire reports citing Matt Kirkland, and which Ohey's write-up does not mention.

== Primary sources
layout: links
link: Polymarket, status page postmortem | https://status.polymarket.com/history/1
link: DebugBear, follow-up statement | https://www.linkedin.com/posts/debugbear_were-continuing-to-investigate-the-security-activity-7522072665495588865-NsiJ
link: Report URI, Polymarket's CSP and the theft | https://report-uri.ghost.io/polymarkets-csp-allowed-every-part-of-the-2-6-million-crypto-theft/
link: Ohey, drainer analysis and timeline | https://ohey.ca/research/polymarket-debugbear-wallet-drainer/
link: Hudson Rock, wallet-draining code in a third-party script | https://www.infostealers.com/article/polymarket-breach-alert-wallet-draining-code-found-in-a-third-party-script/
link: Bitquery, on-chain trace of the drain | https://bitquery.io/investigations/polymarket-hack
link: BleepingComputer, Polymarket halts trading | https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/polymarket-halts-trading-after-crypto-draining-script-breach-25m-traced/
link: Bankless, Polymarket sends $3.9M back | https://bankless.com/read/news/polymarket-sends-3-9m-back-to-traders-after-vendor-script-drain
notes:
These are the primary sources the csoh.org kill chain cites. Every fact about the incident in this deck comes from that page, which draws on these.

Polymarket's status-page postmortem is its own account, and it does not name the vendor. DebugBear's follow-up statement gives its registrar explanation. Report URI reconstructed the CSP and compared the responses. Ohey analyzed the drainer and built a timeline. Hudson Rock retrieved and read the drainer. Bitquery traced the drain on-chain. BleepingComputer reported the trading halt, and Bankless the refunds.

The page also cites DebugBear's initial notice, DeFi Prime, RuntimeWire, Search Engine Journal, CircleID's coverage of a separate campaign, cside, Decrypt on the June 2026 incident, and the MITRE ATT&CK pages for three of its techniques. The background slides name their own sources, from MITRE ATT&CK and OWASP.

